// The manifest for `coordinator` — the directory's name is the namespace every
// verb it ships is registered under (`coordinator_rank`, ...), so this file says
// only what the plugin says about itself.
//
// **This plugin is roles.** It ships no service, keeps no ledger, offers no
// lifecycle handler, starts nothing and watches nothing. Everything the tree
// around it used to do itself now belongs to somebody else: supervision and the
// custody state machine are `eidolon_core::custody` behind the custody doors,
// the words a session can be called by are the operator's `roles.rn`
// (`eidolon_tui::roles`), and the text a session of a given rank is told is
// `ranks/<word>.md` here — read by the custody doors at launch, not by a tool.
//
// What is left is the vocabulary and the one act the vocabulary needs: a word
// put on a session and taken off it again, as a `rank:` tag on the ranked
// session. No role lives in this file and no authority is claimed by it — a
// label or a tag is storage and display, a rank is not a permission, an
// adoption or a reporting line, and the harness reads `rank:` as a string and
// attaches no meaning to it.
pub fn manifest() {
    #{
        name: "coordinator",
        version: "0.3.0",
        description: "the coordinator's rank vocabulary, as roles: the five words (imperator, legatus, tribunus, centurio, miles), the `rank:` tag that publishes one on a session, and the `ranks/<word>.md` blocks the harness appends to a session launched at that rank. The same five words are already core's own role vocabulary, which draws each rank's chip; this plugin ships no role table and nothing here is meant to be copied into `~/.config/eidolon/roles.rn`. No service, no ledger, no lifecycle handler, no supervision: the harness owns all of that",
        claims: #{
            // `rank`/`unrank`/`ranks` set and read a word as a **tag on the ranked
            // session**; they claim no authority over the harness and none over
            // another session. The word is this plugin's own vocabulary and the
            // store is the session's own tags: there is no rank file here, no
            // process behind it and nothing to keep in step with it.
            //
            // There is no `grants` claim either: nothing in this plugin reaches a
            // secret, a file or an endpoint. The monitor that was once declared
            // here on 127.0.0.1:8095 is retired, token and all, and the
            // `lifecycle` block that armed two handlers is gone with it.
            tools: ["rank", "ranks", "unrank"],
        },
        // **The five ranks as launch presets**, which is the whole of what this
        // plugin contributes to a permission decision: a word, the tool classes a
        // session launched at that word holds, and the block it is told. The
        // operator decided these sets; this file is where that decision is written
        // down so the harness can resolve it, and nowhere else.
        //
        // The harness reads this block at admission, refuses the whole plugin if a
        // class name is not one of the seven, a word is not one word, or a prompt path
        // leaves this directory, and then hands the definitions to the one resolution
        // an operator's `--preset`, a custody door and a spawned worker all share
        // (`eidolon_core::preset`). A preset is a **ceiling a brief may narrow**:
        // `--holds` beside it can only take classes away, a worker may only be given
        // a subset of what its parent holds, and no part of this block is read as
        // authority at call time — the dispatcher and the Rune host enforce the
        // session's own resolved set, and this file cannot widen it.
        //
        // The sets are the operator's, class by class: every rank produces or verifies
        // work in the tree, so `fs.read`/`fs.write`/`shell.run` are held by all five;
        // every rank must report up and answer a blocker, so all five hold
        // `peers.message`; `workers.spawn`/`workers.edit` are what separate "does the
        // work" from "runs a team", and a Miles holds neither; `net.reach` is the one
        // coarse class withheld from a bounded executor and from a Centurio, who
        // executes with a team rather than integrating across sources. Above Tribunus
        // the ranks share one set on purpose — the difference there is the brief and
        // who it reports to, which is prompt text, not a capability.
        presets: #{
            "imperator": #{
                capabilities: ["fs.read", "fs.write", "shell.run", "net.reach", "peers.message", "workers.spawn", "workers.edit"],
                prompt: "ranks/imperator.md",
            },
            "legatus": #{
                capabilities: ["fs.read", "fs.write", "shell.run", "net.reach", "peers.message", "workers.spawn", "workers.edit"],
                prompt: "ranks/legatus.md",
            },
            "tribunus": #{
                capabilities: ["fs.read", "fs.write", "shell.run", "net.reach", "peers.message", "workers.spawn", "workers.edit"],
                prompt: "ranks/tribunus.md",
            },
            "centurio": #{
                capabilities: ["fs.read", "fs.write", "shell.run", "peers.message", "workers.spawn", "workers.edit"],
                prompt: "ranks/centurio.md",
            },
            "miles": #{
                capabilities: ["fs.read", "fs.write", "shell.run", "peers.message"],
                prompt: "ranks/miles.md",
            },
        },
    }
}
